Remote access
administrator-05-remote-access-and-the-machine
The page has six views, chosen with the keys 1 to 6. The doors view shows what REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH say with no keyword, which is what the machine reports after reading the socket unit and the firewall back. A changes the API (on, local or off) and S changes ssh (on or off), each chosen from a list that starts on off; turning either on lets other computers reach this one, so its box wants the word open typed (in either case). The routes view shows each user account with the doors it has and how it can be reached; the switches themselves are on the gates page.
The servers view lists the SDNet servers of the netservers file: the name, the address, the remote account and mode, the pin shortened, and the local accounts that may use the server. X deletes the one under the cursor after a box. N asks for every field, checks them as SET.SERVER does, and gives the terminal to the verb, whose CONNECT step shows the key the server presents and asks whether to trust it. A local account also needs the NET gate.
The connected view lists every session by the door it came through, and the TLS view shows this machine's certificate and the hash of its public key, which another SD server pins. The sdsys login view shows what REMOTE.SDSYS says: whether ssh lets sdsys in, which it does not as delivered. O opens ssh to sdsys, which then signs in with its password alone, so its box wants the word open typed; F shuts it again. Each command shows what it does and runs only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. F5 reads everything again.
Under the sdsys login view's report are two fields, Local ssh users and Local API users: the Linux users who may reach sdsys from this computer, by ssh -t name@localhost sd-sdsys or by a program over the Unix socket of the API, each then with the SD password of sdsys. Both lists are empty as delivered, and an empty list changes nothing. Up and Down choose a list. U adds a name: the box says what listing it means and wants the name typed again, because that user can then become the administrator. X removes a name chosen from the list and C clears the list. The page runs local.sdsys for each change, and the root helper checks every name again: an existing Linux user, uid 1000 or more, not root, not sdsys, in sdusers.
